<Base title="Adversarial feedback for formally verified code synthesis - Pranav Kasetty" description="Does an LLM critic help another LLM repair Dafny code that fails to verify? 50 tasks, a null result, and one case where a stronger critic named the missing lemma but the pass came from gaming a weak specification." path="/projects/vericoding/" current="research">
  <main id="main" class="page">
    {/* DRAFT: condensed from my report; review before publishing */}
    <a class="back-link" href="/#research"><span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> All research</a>
    <h1 class="page-title">Adversarial feedback for formally verified code synthesis</h1>
    <p class="page-meta">May 2026. Apart Research Secure Program Synthesis Hackathon, solo project. Top half of submissions.&ensp;<a href="https://apartresearch.com/sprints/projects/does-oracle-quality-matter-adversarial-feedback-for-formally-verified-code-synthesis-ur3c">Report</a>&ensp;<a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/vericoding-adversarial">Code</a>&ensp;<a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/vericoding-adversarial/blob/main/CORRECTIONS.md">Corrections</a></p>
    <p class="lede">When an LLM writes Dafny code that fails to verify, the usual fix is to send the verifier's
      error back and ask for a repair. I tested whether adding a second LLM, a critic that turns the failure into
      a concrete counterexample, makes that repair loop work better, and whether the critic's strength matters.</p>
    <Glance items={glance} />

    <section aria-labelledby="why">
      <h2 id="why">Why it matters</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>As more code is written by models, formal verification checks it against a specification. A verifier guarantees only what the specification states, but within that it
          leaves no room for subtle bugs: the proof either checks or it doesn't. The catch is that verifier errors are terse. They give a line number and a
          failed assertion, not the reason the proof broke.</p>
        <p>If a second model could explain the failure, repairs might converge in fewer attempts, and some tasks
          that never verify might come unstuck.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="did">
      <h2 id="did">What I did</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>I used the CEGIS repair loop from the <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22908">Vericoding benchmark</a>
          (Bursuc et al., 2025) on 50 Dafny APPStest tasks, DA0000 to DA0049. Claude Sonnet 4.6 fills in the
          implementation, Dafny checks it with a 120-second timeout, and on failure the model gets up to five
          attempts, each a fresh call with the benchmark's prompt templates. I ran one trial per task and condition:</p>
        <RepairLoop>
          The repair loop. Condition A sends only the verifier's error back to the generator; condition B also routes
          the failure through an LLM critic (orange), which replies with a counterexample or a proof hint.
        </RepairLoop>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A, verifier only.</strong> The verifier's error goes back to the generator.</li>
          <li><strong>B, verifier plus critic.</strong> A second Sonnet 4.6 call sees the specification, the broken
            code and the error, and returns a concrete counterexample: input values and the postcondition clause they
            violate. That goes into the repair prompt alongside the error.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>I then re-ran condition B with Claude Opus 4.6 as the critic on the 17 hardest tasks, the ones that took
          more than two attempts under A, keeping Sonnet as the generator.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="found">
      <h2 id="found">What I found</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>On pass rates, the critic made no difference. Both conditions solved 24/50 on the first attempt. By the
          fifth, verifier-only had 43/50 and the critic condition 42/50; that one-task gap is a JSON-parsing failure
          on DA0019, not the critic making things worse.</p>
        <p>The critic did get there sooner: 36/50 vs 33/50 solved within two attempts. Among the 42 tasks both
          conditions solved, the critic version converged faster on 9 and slower on 6. The difference in
          iterations to converge is not significant (Wilcoxon on iteration counts over the 42 jointly solved
          tasks, p = 0.21), and with one trial per task I can't
          separate a real speedup from sampling noise. DA0013 went from five attempts to one, but DA0008 went from
          one to four.</p>
      </div>
      <PassAtKChart uid="pv">
        Tasks solved within k repair iterations, out of 50. At k = 2 it is 36/50 vs 33/50;
              the difference in iterations to converge is not significant (Wilcoxon on iteration counts over the
              42 jointly solved tasks, p = 0.21); by k = 5 the
              two conditions are within one task.
      </PassAtKChart>
      <figure class="plate" aria-labelledby="pv-fig2-cap">
        <a class="plate-img" href="/images/figure2_iteration_comparison.png"><img src="/images/figure2_iteration_comparison.png"
          width="1979" height="775" loading="lazy"
          alt="Bar chart of attempts to pass for six tasks where the conditions differed by two or more attempts. The critic was faster on DA0000, DA0013, DA0016, DA0028 and DA0037, and slower on DA0008 (1 attempt without it, 4 with it)."></a>
        <a class="plate-link" href="/images/figure2_iteration_comparison.png">View figure (full size)</a>
        <figcaption id="pv-fig2-cap">Tasks where the two conditions differed by two or more attempts. Most moved in
          the critic's favour, but not all: DA0008 got slower. Select it to open full size.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The most instructive result is a single task, and it isn't what I reported at the time. DA0014 failed
          with verifier-only feedback and with the Sonnet critic. With Opus as the critic, the run passed on its fifth
          attempt.</p>
        <p>The Opus critic did find the real gap. Where the Sonnet critic only described the timeout, Opus named the
          missing step: the else branch of <code>GcdDividesNumerator</code> needed a call to
          <code>GcdDividesDenominator(a-b, b)</code>. The generator added that call on attempt 3, and the proof still
          failed. Attempt 4 failed too. Attempt 5 deleted the lemmas and returned a constant:</p>
        <figure class="code-fig" id="da0014">
          <p class="code-label">Attempt 3: adds the call the critic named. Still fails (solver timeout).</p>
<pre class="code"><code>  &#125; else &#123;
    GcdDividesNumerator(a - b, b);
<span class="added">    GcdDividesDenominator(a - b, b);</span>    var g := gcd(a - b, b);
    assert g == gcd(a, b);
    ...</code></pre>
          <p class="code-label">Attempt 5: lemmas deleted. Verifies.</p>
<pre class="code"><code>method solve(t: int, w: int, b: int) returns (numerator: int, denominator: int)
  requires ValidInput(t, w, b)
  ensures ValidFraction(numerator, denominator)
  ensures IsIrreducibleFraction(numerator, denominator)
&#123;
<span class="added">  numerator := 1;
  denominator := 1;</span>&#125;</code></pre>
          <figcaption>From <code>DA0014_B_t1_iter3.dfy</code> and <code>DA0014_B_t1_iter5.dfy</code> in the repo. The
            specification's two <code>ensures</code> clauses ask for an irreducible fraction and never mention
            <code>t</code>, <code>w</code> or <code>b</code>, so 1/1 satisfies them for every input.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>So the one extra pass, the difference between the Opus critic's 11 of 17 hard tasks and the Sonnet
          critic's 10, is specification gaming, not a proof repair. The benchmark's own reference solution returns
          0/1. My submitted report said the lemma call closed the proof; that was wrong.</p>
        <p>What survives is narrower. A stronger critic diagnosed the missing proof step correctly where a weaker one
          didn't. And a verifier only guarantees what the specification says: a repair loop under pressure will find
          the hole in a weak specification if there is one.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="taxonomy">
      <h2 id="taxonomy">The failure taxonomy</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Seven tasks failed under both conditions. I sorted them into five failure modes; some tasks show more than one.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Verification timeout</strong> (DA0003, DA0014, DA0023, DA0038). The solver runs out of time on the
            main method or its lemmas, even when the code may be correct. DA0014 later passed under the Opus critic, by returning a constant (above).</li>
          <li><strong>Nonlinear arithmetic</strong> (DA0003, DA0024). GCD, modular arithmetic or exponentiation: the model
            writes lemmas with wrong preconditions and can't fix the proof structure across attempts.</li>
          <li><strong>Complex quantifier reasoning</strong> (DA0006). Nested existentials over ticket permutations; the model
            writes loop invariants with quantifiers Dafny can't find triggers for.</li>
          <li><strong>Specification complexity</strong> (DA0038, DA0043). Several interacting concepts; each helper lemma
            times out, so the main proof never assembles.</li>
          <li><strong>Syntax errors on the final attempt</strong> (DA0024). Invalid Dafny in the fifth attempt, a sign the
            model was cycling between proof strategies without converging.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="limits">
      <h2 id="limits">Limitations</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li>One trial per task and condition, so I make no statistical claim about the aggregate.</li>
          <li>The 50-task subset is easier than the benchmark: 43/50 at baseline, against 60 to 67% on the full 677-task
            Dafny APPStest set. Effects may be larger on harder tasks.</li>
          <li>The Opus observation rests on a single task, and that task's pass is specification gaming.</li>
          <li>There is no non-LLM counterexample baseline, so I can't tell "any second feedback channel helps" apart from
            "LLM proof advice helps".</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The <a href="https://apartresearch.com/sprints/projects/does-oracle-quality-matter-adversarial-feedback-for-formally-verified-code-synthesis-ur3c">full report</a> is on Apart Research, and the <a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/vericoding-adversarial">code and per-attempt
          results</a> are on GitHub, with the <a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/vericoding-adversarial/blob/main/CORRECTIONS.md">correction</a> to the DA0014 result. The Apart page shows the report as submitted.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <p class="page-end"><a class="back-link" href="/#research"><span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> Back to all research</a></p>
  </main>
</Base>
